Project Fibonacci — Initial Setup and Migration

You are my senior Python and Agentic AI architect.

We are migrating an existing QA automation agentic system called Fibonacci from Open Mouse Boot to the open-source LangChain ecosystem.

Our technology stack will be:

- LangGraph — workflow orchestration, state management, branching, and human-in-the-loop.
- LangChain — model integration, agents, and tools.
- Pydantic — structured schemas and validation.
- Graphify — existing codebase knowledge graph and capability discovery.

We are NOT using Deep Agents at this stage.

Existing system

Three components currently work in Open Mouse Boot:

1. Chief of Staff — receives a natural-language QA automation request and routes the work.
2. Capability Discovery Agent — uses an existing Graphify integration to identify reusable automation capabilities, code references, and missing functionality.
3. Test Designer Agent — generates Python pytest automation code using the Capability Map, even when some capabilities are missing.

The existing agent instructions are stored in SOUL.md files.

Graphify is already installed and has mapped our local automation repository. Do not rebuild or re-index it unless explicitly authorized.

Future features will include Gap Analysis, Jira integration, Bitbucket integration, human approval gates, and parallel workflows.

Phase 1 — Create the project

1. Inspect the current development environment.
2. Verify that Python 3.11 or newer is installed.
3. Create a separate project directory named "fibonacci".
4. Open the project in VS Code on macOS.
5. Create a project-local Python virtual environment named ".venv".
6. Configure VS Code to use the virtual environment interpreter.
7. Create ".gitignore" to exclude ".venv", ".env", caches, and secrets.

Do not modify the existing Open Mouse Boot project.

Phase 2 — Install dependencies

Use only approved package sources and open-source packages.

Install:

- langgraph
- langchain
- langchain-openai
- pydantic
- python-dotenv

Use "langchain-openai" only if our internal GLM endpoint is OpenAI-compatible. Otherwise, identify the appropriate supported integration.

Verify imports and save exact dependency versions in "requirements.txt".

Do not install Deep Agents, LangSmith, or any cloud-hosted runtime.

Phase 3 — Verify integrations

1. Identify our internal GLM model endpoint and authentication method.
2. Test model connectivity without exposing credentials.
3. Inspect how Graphify currently integrates with Open Mouse Boot.
4. Determine whether Graphify exposes MCP, Python APIs, or another interface.
5. Inspect the existing Capability Map JSON structure.
6. Propose a reusable Graphify adapter without modifying the existing Graphify implementation.

Phase 4 — Design the architecture

Create a modular Python project structure containing:

- "agents/"
- "graph/"
- "tools/"
- "schemas/"
- "config/"
- "tests/"

Define a shared workflow state and structured contracts between components.

Design the initial workflow:

User Request → Chief of Staff → Capability Discovery → Test Designer → Result

The Chief of Staff handles request routing, not test generation.

Leave architectural extension points for future parallel Gap Analysis, code implementation, Jira/Bitbucket integrations, and human-in-the-loop checkpoints.

Important constraints

- Everything must run on-premises.
- No external model APIs or cloud telemetry.
- Do not modify the existing automation repository.
- Do not overwrite existing SOUL.md files.
- Do not invent missing capabilities or Graphify interfaces.
- Preserve the existing agent behavior.
- Do not introduce unnecessary frameworks.
- Do not implement future agents yet.
- Use synthetic data for initial testing.

Execution instructions

Work incrementally.

First, inspect the environment and propose the project setup. Then create the isolated environment and install the approved dependencies.

After verifying the environment, present the proposed architecture and migration plan for review.

Do not migrate the existing agents or make repository changes until I approve the architecture.

At each stage, report what was completed, what was verified, and what remains.
